\begin{proof}
Suppose, toward a contradiction, that $X=U\cup V$ is a separation, where $U$ and $V$ are disjoint nonempty open subsets of $X$. For any $y\in Y$, the fiber $p^{-1}(\{y\})$ is connected. Its intersections with $U$ and $V$ are disjoint relatively open subsets whose union is the fiber. Hence the fiber is contained entirely in $U$ or entirely in $V$.

It follows that $p(U)$ and $p(V)$ are disjoint, nonempty, and cover $Y$. Moreover, since $U=p^{-1}(p(U))$ and $V=p^{-1}(p(V))$, the quotient-map property implies that $p(U)$ and $p(V)$ are open in $Y$. Thus $p(U)$ and $p(V)$ form a separation of $Y$, contradicting the connectedness of $Y$. Therefore $X$ is connected.
\end{proof}
